<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sebastian Lotzer</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1490–after 1525</span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sebastian Lotzer was a furrier's journeyman and lay preacher in Memmingen who wrote <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Reformation</a> pamphlets. In March 1525, as secretary of the Upper Swabian peasant army, he drafted the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/twelve-articles">Twelve Articles</a>, the demands of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/german-peasants-war">German Peasants' War</a>, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christoph-schappeler">Christoph Schappeler</a>. After the rebels' defeat he fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/switzerland">Switzerland</a>, and nothing certain is known of his later life.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sebastian-lotzer.jpg" alt="Sebastian Lotzer" width="320" height="457" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Titelblatt_12_Artikel.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
